INT. PHILOMENA'S KITCHEN - SUNDAY AFTERNOON

A kitchen from another decade. Yellow linoleum, a table with a plastic cloth printed with lemons, a clock shaped like a rooster. On the stove, a pot of sauce simmers, spitting. Steam beads the windowpanes.

PHILOMENA ROSSETTI, 80, sleeves pushed to the elbows, hair in a hairnet, stirs. Her hands are knotted like old rope. At the table, ARTUR ROSSETTI, 50s, her son, in a church shirt, slices bread with surgical care. His daughter SOLEDAD, 16, sits cross-legged on a chair, eyes on her phone, headphones around her neck.

PHILOMENA
You never come on Sundays anymore.

ARTUR
I come on Sundays, Ma.

PHILOMENA
You come on the Sundays you remember.

ARTUR
That's most of them.

PHILOMENA
Most. Hmph.

She tastes the sauce. Adds salt. Stirs.

SOLEDAD
It smells good, Nonna.

PHILOMENA
It smells like your grandfather's lies.

SOLEDAD
What?

PHILOMENA
He swore he could cook. He burned water.

ARTUR
Ma, not today.

PHILOMENA
Why not today?

ARTUR
Because.

PHILOMENA
Because what?

ARTUR
Because it's Sunday.

She snorts. Taps the spoon on the pot.

PHILOMENA
You think I don't know why you've brought the child.

ARTUR
Soledad's here because she wanted to see you.

SOLEDAD
I did.

PHILOMENA
You came to hold my hand while Arturo tells me something he has been practicing in the car.

ARTUR
Ma.

PHILOMENA
Tell me.

Artur sets the knife down. Wipes his fingers. Looks at his daughter, who looks at her phone, pretending not to hear.

ARTUR
The doctor called.

PHILOMENA
Which doctor?

ARTUR
The one with the results. For the house.

PHILOMENA
The house is not sick.

ARTUR
The house is not sick, Ma. You are.

The clock ticks. The sauce pops. Philomena does not turn.

PHILOMENA
I know that.

ARTUR
You know?

PHILOMENA
I am eighty. I have buried a husband, two brothers, and a dog named Salvatore. I know what a cough means.

SOLEDAD
Nonna?

PHILOMENA
Hush, cara.

ARTUR
They want to put you somewhere. Just for a little while. Somewhere with nurses.

PHILOMENA
Somewhere with nurses.

ARTUR
Not forever.

PHILOMENA
They never say forever. They say a little while.

ARTUR
I didn't want to.

PHILOMENA
You didn't want to tell me, or you didn't want to put me in a place?

ARTUR
Both.

She turns now. Her face is wet. It isn't steam. She wipes it on her sleeve.

PHILOMENA
Sit with me. Both of you.

They sit. She lifts the pot, spoons a bowl, sets it before Soledad.

PHILOMENA (CONT'D)
Eat. It gets cold.

SOLEDAD
I'm not hungry.

PHILOMENA
You'll be hungry in a month. When I'm not here to feed you.

She says it lightly. A joke. Nobody laughs.

ARTUR
Ma. Please.

PHILOMENA
Arturo. Your father's recipe. It's in the drawer, under the knives. Never in a book. I wrote it on the back of an electric bill.

ARTUR
I know.

PHILOMENA
You do not know. You think there is a secret ingredient. There isn't. There is only a woman standing here at four o'clock every Sunday for fifty-eight years.

She sits. Folds her hands.

PHILOMENA (CONT'D)
That's the ingredient. Now eat.

Soledad takes a spoonful. Her face crumples. She keeps eating.

Artur reaches across the lemons and takes his mother's hand. It is small and knotted and warm.

The rooster clock ticks. On the stove, the sauce goes on simmering.
